
# LocalCore — Product Blueprint (v0.1 draft)

Windows desktop AI companion. Local-first, offline-capable, cloud strictly optional.
Status: proposal for review. No code, no integrations assumed.

---

## 0. Framing assumptions (please confirm — see Section 5)

These are the assumptions the rest of the document rests on. Each one changes the design if wrong.

1. **One companion persona by default**, with a data model that can later hold multiple user-created characters. (Alternative: multi-character from day one.)
2. **Memory capture is opt-in per category** (conversation facts, transcripts, generated media). Nothing is remembered or persisted unless the user turns it on.
3. **The user's own hardware is the primary runtime.** Cloud is an enhancement, never a requirement for the core loop.
4. **"Desktop presence" means an on-screen avatar window plus tray icon**, not OS-level automation of other apps (that is Phase 5, permissioned).
5. **Existing Electron project is extended, not rewritten.** The VRM renderer, local inference, and conversation memory already in the codebase are kept and wrapped in the interfaces below.

---

## 1. Phased MVP Plan

Guiding rule: **each phase ships something usable and independently testable.** No phase depends on a later one to be worth releasing.

### Phase 0 — Foundation & guardrails
The unglamorous phase that makes every later privacy promise enforceable.

Deliverables:
- Egress Gateway: the single choke point for all network calls.
- Consent & Permission store with defaults = deny.
- Local app-data layout (conversations, memory, assets, logs) with a "no writes outside this dir" guarantee.
- Crash/restart persistence for conversation state.

Acceptance criteria:
- App cold-starts and completes a chat turn **with all outbound network blocked** at the OS level, with no crash and no retry storm.
- No file is written outside the app-data directory (verified by a filesystem watcher over a full session).
- A network call attempted without a granted consent record is blocked and logged, not silently retried.

### Phase 1 — Reliable character & chat
The core loop. This is the phase that must feel *good* before anything else is added.

Deliverables:
- Persona definition (name, voice/tone, backstory, boundaries, refusal style).
- Streaming chat with interruption and cancel.
- Conversation persistence, rename, search, delete.
- Replaceable model provider (at least two providers proven swappable).
- Context assembly: system persona + recent turns + retrieved memory (empty for now).

Acceptance criteria:
- A 50-turn session survives app restart with byte-identical history and order.
- Swapping the model provider mid-session does not corrupt history or duplicate turns.
- Cancelling mid-stream leaves no partial turn persisted and the next turn is clean.
- First token latency and full-response latency measured and reported on target hardware (numbers TBD — see Section 5).
- Deleting a conversation removes its rows and any derived summaries; a second delete is a no-op.

### Phase 2 — Memory you control
Turns "chat" into "companion" — but only with user control.

Deliverables:
- Memory layers: session buffer → rolling summary → long-term facts.
- Facts UI: list, add, edit, delete, with source turn shown.
- Global memory off switch and per-category toggles.
- Export (readable JSON) and full delete.
- Local embedding-based retrieval.

Acceptance criteria:
- Deleting a fact removes it from retrieval within one turn (verified by asking a question that depended on it).
- With memory disabled: no facts are written, and none are injected into any prompt.
- Every retrieved fact shown in a debug view traces to a stored record and a source turn.
- Export produces human-readable JSON; import restores the same retrieval behaviour.
- Memory store is encrypted at rest using OS facilities (Windows DPAPI / safeStorage).

### Phase 3 — Voice
Deliverables:
- Speech-to-text (push-to-talk as the default input).
- Text-to-speech playback with stop/barge-in.
- Always-visible mic indicator and per-session mute.
- Optional voice selection.

Acceptance criteria:
- Push-to-talk produces a transcript and a reply without touching the mouse.
- TTS playback can be interrupted mid-sentence and the companion reacts correctly.
- Mic indicator is visible whenever the mic is open; mute takes effect immediately.
- **No audio is persisted to disk** unless the user explicitly enables transcript/audio saving.
- Measured end-to-end voice round-trip latency reported on target hardware.

### Phase 4 — Avatar expressiveness
Deliverables:
- Emotion states (neutral, happy, thinking, concerned, etc.) driven by conversation cues.
- Lip-sync driven by TTS output.
- Idle animation and blink behaviour.
- Window modes: full window, half-body overlay, tray-only.

Acceptance criteria:
- Each emotion state can be triggered deterministically from a test harness.
- Lip-sync visibly tracks TTS amplitude; mouth closes on silence.
- Idle animation begins after a configurable idle period and stops on interaction.
- Avatar rendering stays within a stated CPU/GPU budget on the target hardware floor.
- Tray-only mode still allows chat and voice.

### Phase 5 — Desktop tools with permissions
Deliverables:
- Tool registry where each tool declares its scope (filesystem roots, shell, screen, etc.).
- Action Broker that mediates every consequential action through an approval prompt.
- Approval UX that shows the exact target and effect (file path, command, diff) before execution.
- Scoped filesystem access limited to user-approved roots.
- Audit log: timestamp, tool, target, decision, result.

Acceptance criteria:
- An action without a granted scope is blocked, not executed, and surfaced to the user.
- The approval dialog shows the exact command/path/diff being approved.
- "Allow once", "Allow for session", and "Always allow" behave distinctly and are revocable.
- Revoking a scope stops the corresponding tool immediately, mid-session.
- Every consequential action appears in the audit log with a matching result entry.
- A destructive action (delete/overwrite) can never proceed on a single implicit approval.

### Phase 6 — Optional cloud & extended presence
Deliverables:
- Explicit cloud enablement, off by default, with a plain-language notice.
- Per-request consent showing exactly what would leave the device (redaction preview).
- Cloud providers behind the same Model Provider interface as local.
- Optional account sync of personas/settings (never memory or media by default).

Acceptance criteria:
- Fresh install performs zero network egress (verified at the OS level).
- Enabling cloud requires an explicit toggle plus an acknowledgment; it cannot be enabled implicitly.
- Each cloud call presents a preview of the payload before sending.
- Disabling cloud returns the app to fully local operation with no residual background calls.
- Sync, if enabled, excludes memory and media unless separately opted in.

